{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788383452081,"sessionID":"ses_f9c0a861affeswRxO5BoLkpFPB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-8c4c391c6a4a73ef","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /tmp/opencode && node -e \"console.log(process.version); try { require('node:sqlite'); console.log('node:sqlite available'); } catch(e) { console.log('node:sqlite NOT available:', e.message); }\""},"output":"v22.23.2\nnode:sqlite available\n(node:295) ExperimentalWarning: SQLite is an experimental feature and might change at any time\n(Use `node --trace-warnings ...` to show where the warning was created)\n","metadata":{"output":"v22.23.2\nnode:sqlite available\n(node:295) ExperimentalWarning: SQLite is an experimental feature and might change at any time\n(Use `node --trace-warnings ...` to show where the warning was created)\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /tmp/opencode && node -e \"console.log(process.version); try { require('node:sqlite'); console.log('node:sqlite available'); } catch(e) { console.log('node:sqlite NOT available:', e.message); }\"","time":{"start":1788383452072,"end":1788383452077}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Node 22.23.2 and npm 10.9.8 are available. TypeScript is not installed globally. Let me set up a TypeScript Node.js project.\n\nI'll build a REST API service for managing books. Let me plan:\n\n1. Use Express as the framework (common, well-supported) or use native Node http. The task says \"Use the specified language and framework\" but framework is \"unknown\". Let me use Express since it's the most common TypeScript Node framework. Actually, to minimize dependencies and potential install issues, I could use native Node http module. But Express is more idiomatic. Let me check if I have internet access for npm install.\n\nLet me think about the approach:\n- TypeScript + Node\n- SQLite: better-sqlite3 is synchronous and great for this. But it requires native compilation. Let me try it - if it fails, I can fall back to node:sqlite (built into Node 22+) or sqlite3.\n\nActually, Node 22 has experimental built-in `node:sqlite` module. That would avoid native compilation. But it's experimental and may emit warnings. Let me check.\n\nActually, let me try a more robust approach. Let me use better-sqlite3 which is widely used. If that fails to install, fall back. Actually, to be safe and avoid native build issues, I could use node:sqlite which is built-in to Node 22.\n\nLet me test what's available. Let me also use Express for the framework.\n\nLet me plan the structure:\n- package.json\n- tsconfig.json\n- src/server.ts (or app.ts)\n- src/db.ts\n- src/routes/books.ts\n- src/types.ts\n- tests/books.test.ts\n- README.md\n\nLet me decide on the testing framework. I'll use vitest or node's built-in test runner. To minimize dependencies, I could use node:test (built-in). That avoids needing to install a test framework. But for HTTP integration tests, I'd need supertest or just use the built-in fetch against the running server.\n\nLet me think about what's simplest and most likely to work:\n- Use node:test (built-in test runner) - no install needed\n- Use express for the framework\n- Use better-sqlite3 or node:sqlite for the DB\n\nLet me first try to install express and better-sqlite3. Actually, let me check internet/npm connectivity first.\n\nLet me design the app to be testable. I'll export the app and a function to create it with a given DB path.\n\nLet me decide on architecture:\n\n**src/types.ts** - Book interface\n**src/db.ts** - DB initialization, returns a Database instance\n**src/app.ts** - Express app factory that takes a db\n**src/server.ts** - starts the server (entry point)\n**tests/books.test.ts** - integration tests using node:test\n\nLet me start. First check npm connectivity.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063f5d91c001YxWdEE2QaoMw8I","sessionID":"ses_f9c0a861affeswRxO5BoLkpFPB","messageID":"msg_063f5ae83001g