{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788383495798,"sessionID":"ses_f9c0a861affeswRxO5BoLkpFPB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"write","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-99cd3eb582769a78","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"content":"{\n  \"name\": \"book-collection-api\",\n  \"version\": \"1.0.0\",\n  \"description\": \"REST API service for managing a book collection (TypeScript + Express + SQLite).\",\n  \"main\": \"dist/src/server.js\",\n  \"type\": \"commonjs\",\n  \"scripts\": {\n    \"build\": \"tsc\",\n    \"start\": \"node dist/src/server.js\",\n    \"typecheck\": \"tsc --noEmit\",\n    \"test\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec dist/tests/\"\n  },\n  \"keywords\": [\"books\", \"rest\", \"api\", \"typescript\", \"express\", \"sqlite\"],\n  \"license\": \"MIT\",\n  \"dependencies\": {\n    \"better-sqlite3\": \"^11.3.0\",\n    \"express\": \"^4.19.2\"\n  },\n  \"devDependencies\": {\n    \"@types/better-sqlite3\": \"^7.6.11\",\n    \"@types/express\": \"^4.17.21\",\n    \"@types/node\": \"^22.5.0\",\n    \"@types/supertest\": \"^6.0.2\",\n    \"supertest\": \"^7.0.0\",\n    \"typescript\": \"^5.5.4\"\n  }\n}\n","filePath":"/workspace/package.json"},"output":"Wrote file successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"filepath":"/workspace/package.json","exists":true,"truncated":false},"title":"package.json","time":{"start":1788383495771,"end":1788383495788}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Wait, I'm using a separate dist-test for tests because the test uses supertest against the express app. But actually, the simplest approach is to write tests in TypeScript and run them with ts-node, OR compile tests with tsc and run with node --test. \n\nLet me reconsider the test setup. Using node:test built-in with compiled JS is fine but the tsconfig needs to output the tests too. Let me use a simpler setup: write tests in TS, and use `tsx` or `ts-node` to run them directly, OR compile everything.\n\nActually, the cleanest approach that's most likely to work reliably: compile all TS (src + tests) to dist/, then run with node --test. Let me set up tsconfig to compile both src and tests into dist/, keeping structure.\n\nLet me restructure: tsconfig.json with outDir \"dist\", rootDir \".\". Then src/server.ts -> dist/src/server.js and tests/books.test.ts -> dist/tests/books.test.js. Then test command: `node --test dist/tests/`.\n\nLet me update package.json accordingly. Actually let me reconsider — having src/ prefix in dist is a bit ugly but fine. Alternatively set rootDir to src and include tests separately. Let me just keep it simple: outDir dist, include [\"src/**/*\", \"tests/**/*\"], and run `node --test dist/tests/`.\n\nLet me rewrite package.json.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063f67d0c001NijRfby1amvH9r","sessionID":"ses_f9c0a861affeswRxO5BoLkpFPB","messageID":"msg_063f64d4e0016iaCeitgap0p0Y"}}
