{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788383655574,"sessionID":"ses_f9c0a861affeswRxO5BoLkpFPB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"bash","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-88878ac5dac25367","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"command":"cd /workspace && grep -n \"prepare\\|interface Statement\\|all(\\|get(\\|run(\\|class Statement\" node_modules/@types/better-sqlite3/lib/index.d.ts | head -40"},"output":"grep: node_modules/@types/better-sqlite3/lib/index.d.ts: No such file or directory\n","metadata":{"output":"grep: node_modules/@types/better-sqlite3/lib/index.d.ts: No such file or directory\n","exit":0,"truncated":false},"title":"cd /workspace && grep -n \"prepare\\|interface Statement\\|all(\\|get(\\|run(\\|class Statement\" node_modules/@types/better-sqlite3/lib/index.d.ts | head -40","time":{"start":1788383655564,"end":1788383655571}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"There are type errors in db.ts. The issue is my generic parameter ordering in `db.prepare<Row, Params>`. The better-sqlite3 types: `prepare<BindParameters, ResultType, ...>`. Let me check the actual signature. The better-sqlite3 `Database.prepare` signature is:\n\n```\nprepare<BindParameters = unknown, ResultType = BindParameters>(sql: string): Statement<BindParameters, ResultType>;\n```\n\nActually the better-sqlite3 types: `Database.prepare<BindParameters = unknown[] | {}, ResultType = Partial<Bindable>>`. Hmm. Let me look at the type definitions.\n\nThe errors suggest that the first generic is being interpreted as the bind parameters, and they don't match. Let me check the actual better-sqlite3 Statement type signature.\n\nFrom @types/better-sqlite3:\n```ts\nclass Database {\n  prepare<BindParameters = unknown[] | {}, ResultType = Partial<Bindable> = ...>(\n    sql: string\n  ): Statement<BindParameters, ResultType>;\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but the error says \"Expected 1 arguments, but got 0\" for `listByAuthor.all({ author })`. And \"Argument of type '{ author: string; }' is not assignable to parameter of type 'BookRow'.\" That means the first generic is being treated as the row type and `.all()` expects that as arg? No...\n\nWait, error TS2554: Expected 1 arguments, but got 0 — that's for `.all()` without args when the statement expects bind params. And the type mismatch `{author}` not assignable to `BookRow` means the generic `<Row, Params>` I wrote got the params bound to BookRow.\n\nActually the issue: I wrote `db.prepare<BookRow, { author: string }>(...)` — the first generic is BindParameters, second is ResultType. So I have it backwards! The first type param is BindParameters (the params), and the second is the ResultType (the row type). So `listByAuthor` = `prepare<{author: string}, BookRow>`. Let me check the .all signature: `all(...bindParameters: BindParameters[]): ResultType[]`. So `.all({author})` should work with BindParameters = {author:string}.\n\nBut the error says `.all()` \"Expected 1 arguments, but got 0\" for `listAll = prepare<BookRow, []>(...)`. So I set BindParameters = BookRow (wrong), and it expects 1 argument of type BookRow... no wait. Hmm, actually `listAll.all()` (no args) — \"Expected 1 arguments, but got 0\" suggests that BindParameters is a tuple of length 1. If BindParameters = BookRow (an object), then `.all()` would require `...bindParameters: BookRow[]` — but that's spread of an array, so it'd accept 0+... Actually `(...bindParameters: T[])` requires at least... no, a rest parameter can be zero. Unless T is a fixed tuple. Hmm.\n\nThis is getting confusing. Let me just check the actual better-sqlite3 .d.ts to write correct generics.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063f8f3f7001UFUbwB8hnUebl8","sessionID":"ses_f9c0a861affeswRxO5BoLkpFPB","messageID":"msg_063f8bce4001ETWEM7z6leL77K"}}
