{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1788383728728,"sessionID":"ses_f9c0a861affeswRxO5BoLkpFPB","part":{"type":"tool","tool":"edit","callID":"chatcmpl-tool-962b294aa61ab299","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"filePath":"/workspace/package.json","newString":"    \"test\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec dist/tests/*.test.js\"","oldString":"    \"test\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec dist/tests/\""},"output":"Edit applied successfully.","metadata":{"diagnostics":{},"diff":"Index: /workspace/package.json\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/package.json\n+++ /workspace/package.json\n@@ -7,9 +7,9 @@\n \"scripts\": {\n   \"build\": \"tsc\",\n   \"start\": \"node dist/src/server.js\",\n   \"typecheck\": \"tsc --noEmit\",\n-  \"test\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec dist/tests/\"\n+  \"test\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec dist/tests/*.test.js\"\n },\n \"keywords\": [\"books\", \"rest\", \"api\", \"typescript\", \"express\", \"sqlite\"],\n \"license\": \"MIT\",\n \"dependencies\": {\n","filediff":{"file":"/workspace/package.json","patch":"Index: /workspace/package.json\n===================================================================\n--- /workspace/package.json\n+++ /workspace/package.json\n@@ -7,9 +7,9 @@\n \"scripts\": {\n   \"build\": \"tsc\",\n   \"start\": \"node dist/src/server.js\",\n   \"typecheck\": \"tsc --noEmit\",\n-  \"test\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec dist/tests/\"\n+  \"test\": \"node --test --test-reporter=spec dist/tests/*.test.js\"\n },\n \"keywords\": [\"books\", \"rest\", \"api\", \"typescript\", \"express\", \"sqlite\"],\n \"license\": \"MIT\",\n \"dependencies\": {\n","additions":1,"deletions":1},"truncated":false},"title":"package.json","time":{"start":1788383728722,"end":1788383728726}},"metadata":{"openrouter":{"reasoning_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"The compiled output is there. The issue is that `node --test dist/tests/` interprets `dist/tests/` as a single test file (with no extension) rather than a directory glob. In Node 22, `node --test [path]` runs the file at that path. To run a directory, I should use a glob pattern or `--test` with a glob. Let me check Node 22's behavior.\n\nActually in Node 22, `node --test` with a path argument: if the path is a directory, it should recursively find test files... Actually the docs say: \"When --test is provided with a path, the test runner will treat the path as a test file.\" Hmm, but there's also the behavior where `node --test` without paths discovers test files. Let me just pass the explicit file path: `node --test dist/tests/books.test.js`. But to be robust and match multiple test files, let me use a glob.\n\nActually the cleanest: `node --test --test-reporter=spec dist/tests/books.test.js`. But better to use a glob that Node expands. Node's `--test` does support glob patterns since Node 21. Let me try `node --test \"dist/tests/**/*.test.js\"`. \n\nActually, let me reconsider. The error shows `node --test ... dist/tests/` tried to require `/workspace/dist/tests` as a module — meaning it treated the trailing-slash path as a file path. So passing a directory path doesn't auto-discover. Let me pass the explicit glob. Node 22 supports `--test` with glob patterns (file spec) when shell expands or Node itself globs.\n\nLet me update the test script to use an explicit glob and let Node handle it. Actually the safest cross-platform approach is to just reference the specific file. But I want it to pick up all *.test.js. Let me use `node --test dist/tests/*.test.js` — Node's `--test` accepts file spec arguments; with a glob, the shell expands it. In bash, `dist/tests/*.test.js` expands to the actual file(s). That works. But if no match, bash passes the literal. With set, fine.\n\nActually, the most reliable: `node --test --test-reporter=spec dist/tests/books.test.js`. Since I only have one test file, that's fine. But to be robust for future tests, let me use the glob and rely on shell expansion. Let me update package.json test script to `node --test --test-reporter=spec dist/tests/*.test.js`. But wait — does `node --test <files>` run them as a test suite rather than requiring them? Yes — `node --test [files...]` runs the specified files as test files. The earlier failure was because I passed a directory `dist/tests/` which Node tried to `require`. Passing actual file paths works.\n\nLet me also verify: with `node --test dist/tests/*.test.js`, bash expands the glob to `dist/tests/books.test.js` and Node runs it. Good.\n\nLet me update the package.json.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063fa1246001UeEH0USlpHMCeB","sessionID":"ses_f9c0a861affeswRxO5BoLkpFPB","messageID":"msg_063f9e053001IAC7oYrDNXTJj0"}}
