_details":[{"type":"reasoning.text","text":"Everything is in place. The `dist/` and `node_modules/` are ignored by .gitignore (they show in `ls -1 -A` but git status doesn't list them as untracked... wait, `git status --short` doesn't show dist or node_modules — because .gitignore handles node_modules/ and dist/. Good. The `_agent_*.log`, `opencode.json`, `stack.json` are pre-existing files not mine; I'll leave them.\n\nLet me do a final confirmation that the build + typecheck + test all pass together cleanly one more time, and present the summary. Actually I already verified all three. \n\nLet me also confirm package-lock.json got created (it did). \n\nLet me update the todo list to mark all complete.\n\nActually, one thing to double check — the `npm test` does `node --test ... dist/tests/*.test.js` which builds? No, it relies on `dist/` being built. I have a `test` script that doesn't build first. The README says run `npm run build` then `npm test`. That's fine but it would be nicer if `test` also built. However, building in test would recompile every run. Let me check: the user said \"Make sure the code builds and tests pass.\" I've verified build passes and tests pass. \n\nTo make `npm test` self-contained, I could chain build then test: `\"test\": \"npm run build && node --test ...\"`. That's a nice touch and ensures tests run against fresh build. Let me do that so that just running `npm test` is sufficient. But the build compiles src AND tests into dist. That's fine.\n\nLet me update the test script to build first.","format":"unknown","index":0}]}},"id":"prt_063fa8efc001SjPjvDyLBHG15U","sessionID":"ses_f9c0a861affeswRxO5BoLkpFPB","messageID":"msg_063fa756c001dOpv98tnBwP4Nc"}}
